the judge will actually collaborate with  the government in coming up with  hypothetical justifications for a law in  order to bend over backwards and uphold  whatever the government is doing so no  you don't get a neutral arbiter hi I'm  Zach Weiss Mulder with reason TV i'm  here with clark Neely he's a senior  attorney at the Institute for justice  and author of the new book terms of  engagement how the courts should enforce  the Constitution's promise of limited  government Clark thank you very much for  talking with us my pleasure thanks for  having me you open the book by talking a  lot about judicial activism and it's a  charge levelled by both the left and the  right often by the right when it's  something they don't like such as a  court ruling in favor of gay marriage or  the left when perhaps the court's ruling  in favor of political speech like the  Citizens United case you talk about not  judicial activism but judicial  engagement judicial activism is  completely overblown in comparison to  the much more serious problem of  judicial abdication and by that I mean  the systemic failure of judges to  genuinely enforce constitutional limits  on government power the opposite of  judicial abdication is judicial  engagement which means it's a sincere  effort on the part of the judges to  enforce constitutional limits on  government power you kind of identify  the point where things went wrong is  when the judiciary started embracing the  rational basis test could you explain  briefly what that is and what's wrong  with it so the rational basis test was  essentially invented during the  progressive movement and really came  into use in the the New Deal when the  courts decided that essentially they  wanted to throw the doctrine of  federalism in other words the idea of a  federal government with limited and  enumerated powers they wanted to throw  that off the back of the constitutional  sled and they also wanted to give the  government a free pass to regulate in  the economy for any reason or no reason  so they invented this thing called the  rational basis test which is actually  none of those things and what it  requires courts to do is essentially  invent justifications for government  regulation even when those  justifications have nothing to do with  the law under review and I just consider  it  to be a paradigmatic example of judicial  abdication the opposite of which which I  already for in the book is judicial  engagement the problem that critics seem  to have with judicial engagement is that  they see it as the judiciary overriding  the will of the people often because  these things have been passed  democratically and then a judge is  swooping in and saying no you this is  not constitutional how do you respond to  that you have a problem with democracy  isn't democracy a good thing democracy  is a good thing in comparison to other  systems of government like  totalitarianism but it is not an  unfettered good and we don't have a pure  democracy in this country we have a  constitutional republic the Constitution  establishes that there are some things  the government may not do and I'll give  you one example that I think most people  respond to in the 20th century more than  half of the states in this country had  laws regarding eugenic sterilization and  we as a country sterilized more than  60,000 people most of them young  minority women I don't think anybody is  going to sit here and say well you know  that's the will of the people and if  those women don't want to be sterilized  they should just go lobby the government  to change that law no we have a  constitution to prevent that kind of  thing and thank goodness an important  part of your argument rests on the  philosophical notion as your kind of  alluding to that individuals have rights  outside of those that are specifically  listed in the Constitution outside of  free speech you know the right to bear  arms there are unenumerated rights where  do you get that idea the idea that the  Constitution contains unenumerated  rights traces right back to the text of  the Constitution so the 9th amendment  specifically says that the enumeration  in the constitution of certain rights  shall not be construed to deny or  disparage other rights retained by the  people this is a clear textual  indication of the existence of onion  umer ated rights this is reinforced by a  provision of the 14th amendment which  was ratified in 1868 to try to help  stamp out the evils of Jim Crow during  the Reconstruction that the privileges  or immunities of citizens of the United  States are not to be infringed by the  states that is a clear reference to  unenumerated rights and  really the only question is what are  they I mentioned one before which is the  right not to be sterilized by the  government for nakedly eugenic purposes  there are others of course and they  would include the right to our living  enter into contracts you identified two  very specific textual examples the 9th  amendment privileges and immunities  clause I've personally never heard the  Ninth Amendment referenced in for  instance a Supreme Court ruling are  these things being ignored by our  judiciary they are being adore the Ninth  Amendment and the fourteenth amendments  privileges or immunities Clause are  systematically ignored by the judiciary  treated as if they didn't exist in the  famous words of now deceased Judge  Robert Bork they are mere inkblots in  the Constitution I think that is a  terribly disrespectful way to approach  the Constitution every part of the  Constitution is meaningful including the  parts that clearly protect onion umer  ated rights like the 9th amendment in  the fourteenth amendments privileges or  immunities Clause inquiry to ignore  those provisions even though they do  you're a working attorney with the  Institute for justice which is a great  organization and everyone should check  it out we'll put up a link so that  everyone can look at it you've seen this  erosion of rights up close and personal  in the courtroom I'm sure the bias that  the court has towards the state what are  some of the examples you've run into in  your own practice it's very difficult to  litigate a rational basis case for  example where you are trying to  vindicate somebody's right whether it's  to earn a living in the occupation of  their choice not to have their home  bulldoze simply to give it to somebody  to build a nicer home because the courts  are so unremittingly pro-government in  these cases and one example that really  stands out for me is when a federal  judge in the state of Louisiana upheld  as a legitimate regulation the Louisiana  law that requires people have a florist  license just to sell floral arrangements  this had a terrible consequence for our  lead client sandy meadows because she  was a widow and a high school dropout  whose only vocational skill was making  floral arrangements and when the state  put her out of business as they did  because she couldn't pass the completely  subjective and arbitrary licensing exam  she was unemployed and she died a few  months later alone  and in poverty because the state of  Louisiana took away her ability to earn  a living in a harmless occupation and I  couldn't persuade a judge to protect her  constitutional right to do that work and  it was it really it was really sad day  from here really ugly sad day judges are  supposed to act as neutral arbiters you  know disinterested third parties when it  comes to a conflict between an  individual and the state is that the  case in America today well it is  sometimes the case that judges act as  neutral arbiters they do so in the  context of rights that the Supreme Court  has deemed fundamental which is simply  constitutional euphemism for important  enough to enforce in a meaningful way  unfortunately that is not the default  setting of our courts the default  setting is that most rights the right to  earn living to own property to eat what  you want to take medicine that you want  all of these are considered to be non  fundamental rights by the court and  therefore not entitled to any meaningful  level of judicial protection instead  they get rational basis review where the  judge will actually collaborate with the  government in coming up with  hypothetical justifications for a law in  order to bend over backwards and uphold  whatever the government is doing so no  you don't get a neutral arbiter in these  so-called non fundamental rights cases  what you essentially get is two  advocates one working for the executive  branch and the other working for the  judiciary who together work together to  come up with a justification for the law  regardless of its true constitutionality  and I think that has to change all of  this rests upon the idea that the  Constitution matters it was written  hundreds of years ago by dead guys who  wore powdered wigs what gives the  constitutional legitimacy and why does  it matter in 2014 in a nutshell I would  say this it's no accident that the US  Constitution is the longest-running  Constitution of any major country in the  world and I think it's because of its  inherent moral legitimacy it's not  perfect but it's about the best that  anybody has ever done and as Randy  Barnett argues in his book restoring the  lost Constitution our Constitution is  binding in conscience because it so  often gets the important questions right  that government should be limited that  individual rights should be respected  and that  government should have to justify its  incursions of liberty to the individual  and not the other way around but  unfortunately the judiciary in the last  80 years or so has completely inverted  that order of values and what we need to  do is to get it back so that the courts  are applying the Constitution the way it  was intended as a bulwark of liberty and  not as a blueprint for the exercise of  government power which is how they 